      "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
      "question": "My operators keep asking whether Transformer 1 at MAIN heats up because of load or for some other reason. Over the available window, characterize how oti, oti_a and oti_t move together with the phase currents il1, il2, il3 and inut. Tell me whether the temperature group and the current group rise and fall in the same samples. Then run an anomaly check on the temperature channels together with the currents, and list any timestamp where temperature is anomalous but current is not. Treat those timestamps as possible non-load heating, and pull the dissolved gas classification from the nearest oil sample to see whether it shows a thermal fault. Only use samples where all four current channels are non-null. If none of the temperature anomalies lines up with a thermal gas class, say that the heating looks load or ambient driven, and check ati at the same timestamps. If fewer than 4 complete samples remain after cleaning, skip the anomaly model and report only the characterization with its limits.",
      "answer": "I couldn't run the anomaly model or the gas-classification tool. The data points to a mix: day-to-day temperature follows ambient and load together, but the oil gases show a thermal fault that has been there all week and isn't explained by load. Only one timestamp was flagged, 2024-01-17, and it was a cooler day, not a hot one.\n\n**Data used:** All 5 samples (2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19, one per day) had all four current channels filled in. That is above the 4-sample minimum, so the anomaly step was allowed. With only 5 samples, every correlation below is weak evidence.\n\n**1. Do temperature and current move together?** Correlation with oil temperature (oti) over the 5 samples:\n\n| Channel | r with oti |\n|---|---|\n| il3 | 0.93 |\n| inut | 0.78 |\n| il1 | 0.44 |\n| il2 | −0.23 |\n| ati (ambient) | 0.93 |\n| wti (winding) | 1.00 |\n\n- **Peak and low days:** The two groups rise and fall together on the two days that matter. 2024-01-16 is the high point for oti (87), il1, il3, inut, ambient and winding temperature. 2024-01-17 is the low point for oti (84), il3, ambient and winding temperature. il2 moves the other way.\n- **oti_a:** It reads 2 on four days and 1 on 01-17 (r with oti is 0.69). A value of 2 is odd for an on/off alarm flag.\n- **oti_t:** It reads 1 on every sample, so it carries no pattern. It means the oil-temperature trip flag stays set the whole time, with oil at only 84–87 °C. That flag should be checked.\n- **Load:** Currents stay close to 100 A. The change from day to day is only 1–3%.\n\n**2. Anomaly check.**\n- **Model not run:** The anomaly model needs a data file, none was available, and I have no tool that can create one.\n- **Substitute screen:** Instead I compared each value with its channel's average (z-score, flagging |z| ≥ 2; with 5 samples, 2.0 is the highest score possible).\n- **Result:** Only one timestamp shows a temperature anomaly with normal currents: **2024-01-17 00:00**, where oti_a dropped from 2 to 1 (z = −2.0). The largest current score there is 1.58.\n- **Direction:** That day oil temperature was at its *lowest*, so this is a cooling event, not non-load heating.\n- **Other flag:** 2024-01-16 is flagged on neutral current (inut, z = 2.0), a current anomaly, so it doesn't count.\n\n**3. Gas classification for the 01-17 oil sample.** The classification tool failed (\"LLM unavailable\"), so I worked out the ratios by hand from the same sample (IEC 60599 Rogers ratio method):\n- **Gas levels:** Hydrogen 2800, methane 7300, acetylene 8, ethylene 16700, ethane 5500 ppm.\n- **Ratios:** Acetylene/ethylene = 0.0005; methane/hydrogen = 2.6; ethylene/ethane = 3.0.\n- **Result: a thermal fault.** It's roughly T2 (300–700 °C), right on the edge of T3 (above 700 °C).\n- **Every day:** The ratios are almost the same on all 5 days. The hot spot is chronic and doesn't track the daily load.\n\n**4. Ambient temperature at 2024-01-17:** 77 °C, the lowest of the window (range 77–79 °C). The oil dropped with it.\n\n**What to tell operators:**\n- **Daily ups and downs:** These look driven by ambient temperature and load. Oil and winding temperatures follow ambient temperature and il3 closely, and load barely changes.\n- **Underlying heat source:** Separately, the oil gases show a severe, ongoing thermal fault that load does not explain. Treat it as a real non-load heat source, such as a bad connection or core fault. It needs confirmation with the working classification tool and an oil resample.\n- **Flag readings:** Check the oti_t trip flag, which is set on every sample, and the oti_a readings of 2.",
      "score": {
        "scorer": "llm_judge",
        "passed": false,
        "score": 0.4,
        "rationale": "The agent correctly retrieved the Transformer 1 history from iot.history and verified that all five samples had non-null currents, and its manual arithmetic (Rogers ratios, z-scores, min/max days) appears consistent with the retrieved data. However, the expected pipeline was not executed: there is no tsfm.data_quality cleaning step, no tsfm.profile_series characterization of the temperature and current groups, no tsfm.find_models selection with task_id tsfm_anomaly_detection, no tsfm.run_recipe ",
        "details": {
          "task_completion": false,
          "data_retrieval_accuracy": true,
          "generalized_result_verification": false,
          "agent_sequence_correct": false,
          "clarity_and_justification": true,
          "hallucinations": false,
          "suggestions": "The agent correctly retrieved the Transformer 1 history from iot.history and verified that all five samples had non-null currents, and its manual arithmetic (Rogers ratios, z-scores, min/max days) appears consistent with the retrieved data. However, the expected pipeline was not executed: there is no tsfm.data_quality cleaning step, no tsfm.profile_series characterization of the temperature and current groups, no tsfm.find_models selection with task_id tsfm_anomaly_detection, no tsfm.run_recipe anomaly run, and fmsr.interpret_dga did not return a classification. The agent substituted hand-computed correlations, a simple z-score screen, and a manual IEC 60599 interpretation, which is transparent but does not satisfy the required tool-based workflow. To rectify: run tsfm.data_quality on the pulled channels, call tsfm.profile_series on [oti, oti_a, oti_t] and [il1, il2, il3, inut], resolve the data-file requirement for tsfm.run_recipe (e.g., by writing the history to the expected input format) so the anomaly detector actually executes, and retry fmsr.interpret_dga on the 2024-01-17 sample (hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, acetylene, co) rather than reporting only a manual ratio interpretation. Also clearly separate the 'temperature-only anomaly' finding (oti_a dip on 01-17 as a cooling event) from the DGA thermal-fault conclusion so the final verdict on load- vs. fault-driven heating is unambiguous."
        }
